<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rasht</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rasht is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> province in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. It became the chief city of Gilan in the Safavid period and grew rich from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1920 it was the seat of the short-lived Persian Socialist Soviet Republic proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-kuchik-khan">Mirza Kuchik Khan</a> and his allies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rasht.jpg" alt="Rasht" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexTrn1991 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rasht_Municipality_Mansion_in_an_Autumn_night.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rasht is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> province, of Rasht County and of its district, and the largest city on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s Caspian Sea coast. It lies between the coast and the mountains, and the climate is humid subtropical and among the wettest in Iran, with average humidity above 80 percent. A temperate rainforest lies to its south, in contrast to the mostly arid rest of the country. Its nickname is the City of Silver Rain. The name has several explanations: the word resh, meaning very light rain; the verb reshtan, to spin, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> thread trade; or a low, sunken place, from an early market called rasht bazar. The people are mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilaks">Gilaks</a>, who speak the Rashti variety of Western Gilaki.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Rasht was first mentioned in Umayyad historical documents in 682 CE. The Hudud al-'Alam, a tenth-century geography, names it as a district. Its modern history dates to the Safavid era, when it was a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade with many textile workshops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepan-razin">Stenka Razin</a> plundered it in 1669, an earthquake destroyed it in 1714, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> occupied it from 1722 to 1732. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> period it became the gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, trading with Russia through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-e-anzali">Bandar-e Anzali</a>; Russian, British, French and Ottoman consulates opened there. A Russian visitor at the end of 1860 counted 5,463 houses, 22 mosques and 27,314 people. The plague devastated the city in 1901.</p><p>People of Rasht played a prominent part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution</a> of 1905–1907, and Mīrzā Kūchak Khān, a leader of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangal-movement">Jangali movement</a>, was born there. In 1920, after the defeat of the constitutional forces, he and Iranian communists established a short-lived socialist republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>, supported at first by the Russian Red Army, with its capital at Rasht. The Soviet government withdrew its support, internal conflicts divided the republic, and the Iranian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> defeated it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s first public library, first girls' school and first fire station are said to have been built in Rasht, and the Nasim-e-Shomal newspaper was first published there. Its first university was established in 1974.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 679,995 people in the city, the most populous in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Rasht is a major trade centre between Caucasia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Iran, using the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-e-anzali">Bandar-e Anzali</a>, and the surrounding plain is the most important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing region of Iran. The resort village of Masouleh lies in the nearby mountains. In 2015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> admitted Rasht to its network of creative cities for gastronomy. Its cuisine includes Mirza Ghassemi, and Rasht embroidery is a local handicraft. The Rosa 'de Rescht', a rose reported in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at the end of the nineteenth century, was rediscovered near the city by Nancy Lindsay in 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rasht” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From around the 14th century the Ishaqids, who claimed descent from the prophet Isaac, ruled Biyapas, with their centres at Fuman and later <b>Rasht</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ishaqid-dynasty">Ishaqid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Hedayat-Allah Khan, of a notable family from Fuman, became governor of Gilan under Karim Khan of the Zand Empire around 1762 and soon ruled the province as an autonomous khanate with its centre at <b>Rasht</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-gilan">Khanate of Gilan</a></li><li><blockquote>With Soviet support, Kuchik Khan proclaimed the Persian Socialist Soviet Republic at <b>Rasht</b> in June 1920.</blockquote><a href="/state/persian-socialist-soviet-republic">Persian Socialist Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
